== Relations Pleasing to the Godly

XII. Nothing can be concluded with certainty about the nature of the religious affections anyone has from this, that their outward manifestations, and the account persons give of them, are very affecting and pleasing to the truly godly, and such as greatly gain their charity and win their hearts.

The true saints do not have such a spirit of discernment that they can determine with certainty who are godly and who are not. For though they know by experience what true religion is in its inward exercises, these are what they can neither feel nor see in the heart of another.#footnote[“Men may have the knowledge of their own conversion; the knowledge other men have of it is uncertain, because no man can look into the heart of another and see the workings of grace there.” #emph[Stoddard’s] Nat of saving Conv. Chapter 15. At the beginning.] Nothing in others comes within their view but outward manifestations and appearances. But the Scripture plainly suggests that this way of judging what is in men by outward appearances is uncertain at best and liable to deceive: “The LORD does not see as man does. For man sees the outward appearance, but the LORD sees the heart” (1 Sam 16:7). “He will not judge by what His eyes see, and He will not decide by what His ears hear” (Isa 11:3).#footnote[Mr. #emph[Stoddard] observes that #emph[all visible signs are common to converted and unconverted men,] and an #emph[account of experiences] among the rest. #emph[Appeal to the learned,] p. 75. “O how hard it is for the eye of man to discern between chaff and wheat! And how many upright hearts are now censured whom God will clear! How many false hearts are now approved whom God will condemn! Men ordinarily have no convincing proofs, but only probable signs, which at most produce only a conjectural knowledge of another’s state. And those who judge absolutely either way may possibly wrong the generation of the upright, or on the other side, acquit and justify the wicked. And truly, considering what has been said, it is no wonder that dangerous mistakes are made so often in this matter.” #emph[Flavel’s] Husb. Spir. Chapter 12.] Those who are quick and absolute in deciding persons’ states, boasting of their extraordinary gift of discerning and distinguishing in these great matters, as though all was open and clear to them, are commonly only poor judges and dangerous counselors in cases of the soul. They betray one of these three things: either that they have had only a little experience, or that they are persons of weak judgment, or that they have a great degree of pride and self-confidence, and so of ignorance of themselves. Wise and experienced men will proceed with great caution in such a matter.

When there are many probable appearances of piety in others, it is the duty of the saints to receive them heartily into their charity, and to love them and rejoice in them as their brethren in Christ Jesus. But even so, the best of men may be deceived when the appearances seem to them exceedingly fair and bright, even so as to gain their charity entirely and conquer their hearts. It has been a common thing in the church of God for such bright professing Christians, who are received among the saints as eminent saints, to fall away and come to nothing.#footnote[“Do not be offended if you see great cedars fall, stars fall from heaven, great professing Christians die and decay. Do not think they are all such. Do not think that the elect will fall. Truly, some are such that when they fall, one would think a man truly sanctified might fall away, as the #emph[Arminians] think. ‘They did not belong to us’ (1 John 2:19). I speak this because the Lord is shaking, and I look for great apostasies; for God is testing all his friends throughout the Christian world. In #emph[Germany] what profession there was! Who would have thought it? The Lord, who delights to show openly what was hidden in secret, sends a sword, and they fall.” #emph[Shepard’s] Parab. Part I. P. 118, 119. “The saints may approve you, and God condemn you: ‘You have a reputation for being alive, yet you are dead’ (Rev 3:1). Men may say, ‘There is a true #emph[Nathanael,]’ and God may say, ‘There is a self-deceiving #emph[Pharisee.]’ Reader, you have heard of #emph[Judas] and #emph[Demas,] of #emph[Ananias] and #emph[Sapphira,] of #emph[Hymenaeus] and #emph[Philetus,] once renowned and famous professing Christians, and you have heard how they turned out at last.” #emph[Flavel’s] touchstone of sincerity, ch. 2. Sect. 5.]. And we need not wonder at this if we consider the things already observed, the things it has been shown may appear in men who are altogether graceless. Nothing prevents all these things from meeting together in men while they are without a spark of grace in their hearts. They may have religious affections of many kinds together. They may have a sort of affection toward God that closely resembles dear love to him, and so a kind of love to the brethren, and great appearances of wonder at God’s perfections and works, and sorrow for sin, and reverence, submission, self-abasement, gratitude, joy, religious longings, and zeal for the interest of religion and the good of souls. And these affections may come after great awakenings and convictions of conscience. There may be great appearances of a work of humiliation, and counterfeit love and joy and other affections may seem to follow these, and one another, in just the same order that is commonly observed in the holy affections of true converts. And these religious affections may be carried to a great height. They may cause an abundance of tears, indeed may overcome the bodily nature of those who have them, and may make them full of feeling, fervent and fluent in speaking of the things of God, and incline them to do it abundantly. They may be accompanied by many sweet texts of Scripture and precious promises brought to their minds with great impression. And they may incline them to praise and glorify God with their mouths in a very ardent way, and fervently to call on others to praise him, crying out about their unworthiness and exalting free grace. And moreover they may incline them to abound in the external duties of religion, such as prayer, hearing the word preached, singing, and religious conversation. And these things may be accompanied by a great resemblance of a Christian assurance in its greatest height, when the saints mount up on eagles’ wings above all darkness and doubting. I think it has been made plain that all these things may be there, and yet there may be nothing more than the common influences of the Spirit of God, joined with the delusions of #emph[Satan] and the wicked and deceitful heart. To this I may add that all these things may be accompanied by a sweet natural temper, a good doctrinal knowledge of religion, a long acquaintance with the saints’ way of talking and of expressing their affections and experiences, a natural ability and skill in suiting their expressions and manner of speaking to the inclinations and notions of the hearers, and an attractive propriety of expression and behavior formed by a good upbringing. How great, therefore, the resemblance may be, in all outward expressions and appearances, between a hypocrite and a true saint! Doubtless it is the glorious prerogative of the all-knowing God, as the great searcher of hearts, to be able to separate well between sheep and goats. And what unseemly self-exaltation and arrogance it is in poor, fallible, dark mortals to claim that they can determine and know who are really sincere and upright before God, and who are not!

Many seem to lay great weight on this, and to suppose it to be what may settle the question of others’ real piety: when they not only tell a plausible story, but in giving an account of their experiences they present things and speak in such a way that the hearers #emph[feel] their talk. That is to say, their talk seems to harmonize with the hearers’ own experience, and the hearers’ hearts are touched and affected and delighted by what they hear them say, and drawn out by it in dear love to them. But there is not the certainty in such things, or the full dependence to be placed on them, that many imagine. A true saint greatly delights in holiness. It is a most beautiful thing in his eyes, and God’s work in savingly renewing a poor soul that was perishing before, and making it holy and happy, appears to him a most glorious work. No wonder, therefore, that his heart is touched and greatly affected when he hears another give a probable account of this work done in his own heart, and when he sees probable appearances of holiness in him, whether those pleasing appearances have anything real to answer them or not. And if he uses the same words commonly used to express the affections of true saints, and tells of many things following one another in an order that agrees with the method of the experience of the one who hears him, and also speaks freely and boldly and with an air of assurance, no wonder the other thinks his experiences harmonize with his own. And if, besides all this, he speaks with much affection in giving his account, and above all, if in speaking he seems to show much affection to the one to whom he speaks, such an affection as the #emph[Galatians] had to the apostle #emph[Paul,] these things will naturally have a powerful influence to affect and draw the hearer’s heart, and open wide the doors of his charity toward him. #emph[David] speaks as one who had felt #emph[Ahithophel’s] talk, and had once found a sweet savor and relish in it. And therefore his surprise and disappointment were exceedingly great when he fell; it was almost too much for him: “For it is not an enemy who insults me; that I could endure. … But it is you, a man like myself, my companion and close friend. We shared sweet fellowship together; we walked with the crowd into the house of God” (Ps 55:12–14).

It is with professing Christians, especially those who become so in a time of the outpouring of the Spirit of God, as it is with the blossoms in the spring.#footnote[A time of the outpouring of the Spirit of God, reviving religion and producing its pleasant appearances in new converts, is compared in Scripture to this very thing, #emph[namely,] the spring season, when the kindly influences of the heavens cause the blossoms to put forth. Song 2:11, 12.] There are vast numbers of them on the trees, which all look fair and promising, but even so, very many of them never come to anything. And many of those that in a little while wither up and drop off and rot under the trees look as beautiful and bright as the others for a while. And not only so, but they smell sweet and send out a pleasant fragrance. So we cannot with certainty, by any of our senses, distinguish the blossoms that have in them the secret virtue which will appear afterwards in the fruit, and the inward solidity and strength which will enable them to bear and cause them to be perfected by the hot summer sun that will dry up the others. It is the mature fruit which comes afterwards, and not the beautiful colors and smell of the blossom, that we must judge by. So new converts (professedly so) may appear fair in their talk about the things of religion, and be very savory, and the saints may think they talk with real feeling. They may relish their talk and imagine they perceive a divine savor in it, and yet all may come to nothing.

It is strange how hard it is to bring men to be content with the rules and directions Christ has given them. They must go by other rules of their own invention, which seem to them wiser and better. I know of no directions or counsels Christ ever gave more plainly than the rules he has given us to guide us in judging others’ sincerity, #emph[namely,] that we should judge the tree chiefly by its fruit. But even so, this will not do, and other ways are found out which are imagined to be more distinguishing and certain. And woeful have been the harmful consequences of this arrogant setting up of men’s wisdom above the wisdom of Christ. I believe many saints have gone far out of the way of Christ’s word in this respect, and some of them have been chastised with whips and (I had almost said) scorpions to bring them back again. But many things that have appeared lately, and appear now, may convince us that ordinarily those who have gone furthest in this way, who have been most highly conceited of their gift of discerning, and have appeared most eager to decide the state of men’s souls absolutely and suddenly, have been hypocrites who have known nothing of true religion.

In the parable of the wheat and tares it is said, “When the wheat sprouted and bore grain, then the tares also appeared” (Matt 13:26). It is as though the tares were not discerned or distinguishable from the wheat #emph[until then,] as Mr. #emph[Flavel] observes.#footnote[Husbandry spiritualized, ch. 12.] He mentions it as an observation of #emph[Jerome’s] that #emph[wheat and tares are so much alike, until the blade of the wheat comes to bring forth the ear, that it is next to impossible to distinguish them.] And then Mr. #emph[Flavel] adds, “However difficult it is to discern the difference between wheat and tares, yet doubtless the eye of sense can distinguish them much more easily than the quickest and most piercing eye of man can discern the difference between special and common grace. For all saving graces in the saints have their counterfeits in hypocrites. There are similar works in them which a spiritual and very judicious eye may easily mistake for the saving and genuine effects of a sanctifying Spirit.”

As it is the ear or the fruit which distinguishes the wheat from the tares, so this is the true #emph[Shibboleth] which he who stands as judge at the fords of the #emph[Jordan] uses to distinguish those who will pass over #emph[Jordan] into the true #emph[Canaan] from those who were to be slain at the fords. For the Hebrew word #emph[Shibboleth] means an ear of grain. And perhaps the fuller pronunciation of #emph[Jephthah’s] friends, #emph[Shibboleth,] may represent a full ear with fruit in it, typifying the fruits of the friends of Christ, the antitype of #emph[Jephthah.] And the thinner pronunciation of the #emph[Ephraimites,] his enemies, may represent their empty ears, typifying the show of religion in hypocrites, without substance and fruit. This agrees with the doctrine we are abundantly taught in Scripture, #emph[namely,] that he who is appointed to judge those who pass through death, whether they have a right to enter the heavenly #emph[Canaan] or not, or whether they should be slain, will judge every man according to his works.

We seem to be taught the same things by the rules given for the priests’ discerning of leprosy. In many cases it was impossible for the priest to determine whether a man had leprosy or was clean by the closest inspection of the appearances on him, until he had waited to see what the appearances would come to, and had shut up the person who showed himself to him for one seven days after another. And when he judged, he was to decide by the hair which grew out of the spot shown to him, which was as it were the fruit it brought forth.

And here, before I finish what I have to say under this head, I would say something about a strange notion some have lately been led away with, of knowing with certainty the good state others are in, as though it were revealed to them immediately from heaven by their love flowing out to them in an extraordinary way. They argue like this. Their love being very great and deeply felt, it may be known with certainty by those who feel it to be a true Christian love. And if it is a true Christian love, the Spirit of God must be its author. And since the Spirit of God, who knows with certainty whether others are the children of God or not, and is a spirit of truth, is pleased by an uncommon influence on them to cause their love to flow out in an extraordinary way toward such a person as a child of God, this infallible Spirit, who deceives no one, must know that that person is a child of God. But such persons might be convinced that their reasoning is false if they would consider this: is it not their duty, and what God requires of them, to love as the children of God those whom they think are the children of God, and whom they have no reason to think otherwise of from all they can see in them, even though God, who searches the hearts, knows that they are not his children? If it is their duty, then it is good, and the lack of it is sin. And therefore surely the Spirit of God may be its author. The Spirit of God, without being a spirit of falsehood, may in such a case help a person to do his duty and keep him from sin. But then they argue from the uncommon degree and special manner in which their love flows out to the person, which they think the Spirit of God would never cause if he did not know the object to be a child of God. But then I would ask them whether it is not their duty to love to a very great degree all those whom they are bound to think are the children of God from all they can see in them, though God, from other things he sees which are out of their sight, knows that they are not. It is men’s duty to love all whom they are bound in charity to look on as the children of God with a vastly dearer affection than they commonly do. As we ought to love Christ to the utmost capacity of our nature, so it is our duty to love those whom we think are so near and dear to him as his members with an exceedingly dear affection, as Christ has loved us. And therefore it is sin in us not to love them so. We ought to pray to God that by his Spirit he would keep us from sin and enable us to do our duty. And may not his Spirit answer our prayers and enable us to do our duty in a particular instance without lying? If he cannot, then the Spirit of God is bound not to help his people do their duty in some instances, because he cannot do it without being a spirit of falsehood. But surely God is sovereign enough for that: he may enable us to do our duty when he pleases and on whatever occasion he pleases. When persons think others are his children, God may have other ends in causing their exceedingly endeared love to flow out to them besides revealing to them whether their opinion of them is right or not. He may have this merciful end in it, to enable them to do their duty and to keep them from that dreadful, infinite evil, sin. And will they say that God may not show them that mercy in such a case? Suppose I am away from home and hear that in my absence my house has burned down, but my family have all escaped the flames in some extraordinary way, and everything in the circumstances of the story as I hear it makes it appear very credible. In such a case it would be sin in me not to feel a very great degree of gratitude to God, even though the story is in fact not true. And is not God so sovereign that he may, if he pleases, show me that mercy on that occasion, and enable me to do my duty to a much greater degree than I used to, and yet not incur the charge of deceitfulness in confirming a falsehood?

It is exceedingly plain from Romans 14:6 that error or mistake may be the occasion of a gracious exercise, and consequently of a gracious influence of the Spirit of God: “He who eats does so to the Lord, for he gives thanks to God; and he who abstains does so to the Lord and gives thanks to God.” The apostle is speaking of those who, through erroneous and needless scruples, avoided eating foods that were ceremonially unclean. By this it is very evident that there may be true exercises of grace, a true regard #emph[to the Lord,] and particularly a true thankfulness, which may be occasioned both by an erroneous judgment and by an erroneous practice. And consequently an error may be the occasion of those truly holy exercises that are from the infallible Spirit of God. And if so, it is certainly too much for us to determine to how great a degree the Spirit of God may give this holy exercise on such an occasion.

This notion of discerning another’s state with certainty by love flowing out is not only not founded on reason or Scripture, but it is unscriptural. It is against the rules of Scripture, which say not a word of any such way of judging the state of others as this, but direct us to judge chiefly by the fruits that are seen in them. And it is against the doctrines of Scripture, which plainly teach us that the state of others’ souls toward God cannot be known by us, as in Revelation 2:17: “To the one who overcomes, I will give the #emph[hidden manna.] I will also give him a white stone inscribed with a new name, #emph[known only to the one who receives it.]” And Rom 2:29: “A man is a Jew because he is one inwardly, and circumcision is a matter of the heart, by the Spirit, not by the written code. Such a man’s praise does not come from men, but from God.” By this last expression, #emph[such a man’s praise does not come from men, but from God,] the apostle refers to the inability of men to judge whether a man is inwardly a #emph[Jew] or not (as they could easily see by outward marks whether men were outwardly #emph[Jews]), and means that it belongs to God alone to give the deciding voice in this matter. This is confirmed by the same apostle’s use of the phrase in 1 Corinthians 4:5: “Therefore judge nothing before the appointed time; wait until the Lord comes. He will bring to light what is hidden in darkness and will expose the motives of men’s hearts. #emph[At that time each will receive his praise from God.]” The apostle, in the two verses before, says, “I care very little, however, if I am judged by you#super[+] or by any human court. In fact, I do not even judge myself. My conscience is clear, but that does not vindicate me. It is the Lord who judges me.” And again, it is further confirmed because in this second chapter to the #emph[Romans] the apostle directs his speech especially to those who had a high conceit of their own holiness, made their boast of God, and were confident of their own discernment. They were confident that they knew God’s will and approved the things that were excellent, or tested the things that differ (as it is in the margin) (verse 18), #emph[and were convinced that they were guides for the blind, a light for those in darkness, instructors of the foolish, teachers of infants,] and so they took it on themselves to judge others; see verse 1 and verses 17–20.

And how arrogant the notion must be of those who imagine they can know others’ godliness with certainty, when that great apostle #emph[Peter] claims to say no more about #emph[Silvanus] than that he was #emph[a faithful brother, as he supposed] (1 Pet 5:12). And yet this #emph[Silvanus] appears to have been a very eminent minister of Christ, an evangelist, a famous light in God’s church at that time, and an intimate companion of the apostles. See 2 Cor 1:19; 1 Thess 1:1; 2 Thess 1:1.
